[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Concurrency Control in Java: Locks, ReadWriteLock, StampedLock, and Condition Variables"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Effective concurrency management is crucial for high-performance Java applications. While synchronized blocks and methods provide basic thread safety, the java.util.concurrent.locks package offers advanced mechanisms for fine-grained synchronization, improved throughput, and complex thread coordination."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. ReentrantLock: Basic Mutex with Advanced Capabilities"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The ReentrantLock class implements the Lock interface and provides the same basic behavior and semantics as the implicit monitor lock accessed using synchronized methods and statements, but with extended capabilities such as timed lock waits, interruptible lock waits, and fairness policies."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class Counter {
    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private int count = 0;

    public void increment() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            count++;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            return count;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. ReadWriteLock: Optimizing Read-Heavy Scenarios"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"A ReadWriteLock maintains a pair of associated locks, one for read-only operations and one for writing. The read lock may be held simultaneously by multiple reader threads, so long as there are no writers. The write lock is exclusive. This drastically improves concurrency in structures where reads vastly outnumber writes."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReadWriteLock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock;

public class CachedData {
    private final Map<String, Object> map = new TreeMap<>();
    private final ReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();

    public Object get(String key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            return map.get(key);
        } finally {
            lock.readLock().unlock();
        }
    }

    public void put(String key, Object value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            map.put(key, value);
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"3. StampedLock: Ultra-Fast Optimistic Locking"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Introduced in Java 8, StampedLock controls access to a resource through locks which can be acquired in one of three modes: Writing, Reading, and Optimistic Reading. Optimistic reading does not actually acquire a lock; instead, it returns a stamp and validates that no write operations have occurred since the stamp was issued."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.locks.StampedLock;

public class Point {
    private double x, y;
    private final StampedLock sl = new StampedLock();

    void move(double deltaX, double deltaY) {
        long stamp = sl.writeLock();
        try {
            x += deltaX;
            y += deltaY;
        } finally {
            sl.unlockWrite(stamp);
        }
    }

    double distanceFromOrigin() {
        long stamp = sl.tryOptimisticRead();
        double currentX = x, currentY = y;
        if (!sl.validate(stamp)) {
            stamp = sl.readLock();
            try {
                currentX = x;
                currentY = y;
            } finally {
                sl.unlockRead(stamp);
            }
        }
        return Math.sqrt(currentX * currentX + currentY * currentY);
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"4. Condition Variables: Precise Thread Signaling"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Condition instances are bound to Lock instances. To get a Condition instance for a given Lock instance, use the newCondition() method. Condition factors out the Object monitor methods (wait, notify, and notifyAll) into distinct objects to give the effect of having multiple wait-sets per object, by combining them with the use of arbitrary Lock implementations."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.locks.Condition;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class BoundedBuffer {
    final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    final Condition notFull  = lock.newCondition(); 
    final Condition notEmpty = lock.newCondition(); 

    final Object[] items = new Object[100];
    int putptr, takeptr, count;

    public void put(Object x) throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (count == items.length)
                notFull.await();
            items[putptr] = x;
            if (++putptr == items.length) putptr = 0;
            ++count;
            notEmpty.signal();
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public Object take() throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (count == 0)
                notEmpty.await();
            Object x = items[takeptr];
            if (++takeptr == items.length) takeptr = 0;
            --count;
            notFull.signal();
            return x;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices and Summary"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always wrap lock acquisition and modifications in a try-finally block to ensure locks are always released."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use ReentrantLock for standard mutual exclusion with features like interruptible waits."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use ReadWriteLock when reads significantly outnumber writes."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use StampedLock for maximum read performance via optimistic locking, but be aware it is not reentrant."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Condition variables to replace wait/notify mechanisms with multiple waiting queues per lock."}]
